huddled frightened and packed into  border force boats more than a hundred  migrants made it across the channel  today and that comes after 254 people  across the channel on sunday according  to the ministry of defense's latest  figures in just seven boats  it gives you an idea of just how  dangerously overcrowded those small  dinghies are  but they keep on coming it's expected  that the number of migrants intercepted  in the english channel today will  surpass the 254 of yesterday  it's not just young men who the  government say make the crossing is  economic migrants some are very frail  and need to be helped by border force  guards and children and babies are among  those on the boats  it's warm here in dover the sea behind  me is flat and calm ideal conditions you  might think for a bank holiday crossing  if you were on a ferry or a yacht  however in small flimsy boats it's still  incredibly dangerous but that's not  enough to put some people off making the  treacherous crossing  nor is the government's nationality and  borders bill which became law on  thursday and makes it a criminal offence  to knowingly arrive in the uk illegally  the government hopes that in a matter of  weeks it will be able to send migrants  like these to rwanda to apply for asylum  in that country the policy was supposed  to be deterrent for those risking the  journey  critics say the reality is the  government hasn't given people  alternative routes leaving them  vulnerable to exploitation it's a cruel  inhumane approach to expel people to  rwanda to treat them as nothing more  than human  cargo they have no choice people that  are trying to reach the uk they have  other no other safe route to get to the  uk if you're fleeing the taliban what  are you supposed to do a government  spokesperson said over the weekend the  rise in dangerous channel crossings is  unacceptable  not only are they an overt abuse of our  immigration laws but they also impact on  the uk taxpayer risk lives and our  ability to help refugees come to the uk  by safe and legal routes  there has been increased surveillance in  the channel there have been drones as  well as royal navy patrols  but there's still a lack of cooperation  and disagreement between the french and  british border forces and caught in the  middle prayed on by smugglers are the  desperate people with no other options  madeleine ratcliffe sky news in dover
 

